"""SE423 Chapter 1.2: notes that explain instead of repeating the slide.

Each list replaces that slide's notes in ch01_2_page.py and must keep the same length and order, because highlights
link to note numbers. A note says what a point means, why it matters, and gives an example.
"""

NOTES = {
    2: ['Think of a domain as one area a PM must keep an eye on, such as “are the stakeholders on board?”. It is a **group of related activities** rather than a single task, and it is **critical** because neglecting it damages the outcome, whatever else goes well.',
        'Interdependent means a problem in one area spreads. If nobody engages stakeholders, requirements come in wrong, so planning is built on bad information and delivery gets rejected. That’s why the domains are **interactive, interrelated and interdependent** rather than separate checklists.',
        'You never “finish” a domain and move on. On the last day of a project you are still managing stakeholders, measuring and handling uncertainty. So they **run concurrently** whether you ship weekly or once at the end. They are **not** phases done in order, which is exactly the trap in the real MCQ.'],
    3: ['The definition is deliberately broad. A neighbor annoyed by construction noise is a stakeholder, even with no role in the project. So is someone who only *believes* the project will take their job: **perceive itself to be affected** counts, because their reaction can still affect you.',
        'You can’t give everyone equal attention, so **stakeholder analysis** works out who matters and how. Numbers such as budget influence, plus judgment such as attitude toward the project, tell you **whose interests to take into account** first.',
        'Teams tend to list only friendly people inside the company. Include people **internal and external**, and especially those who **oppose** the project: a regulator or an unhappy department can block you late, when change is most expensive.'],
    7: ['One person often does both jobs in the same meeting. Fixing the agenda and the timeline is **management**, while getting a discouraged tester back on board is **leadership**. A project needs both kinds of effort, so neither one replaces the other.',
        '**Management** is about the machinery: are the plan, the tracking and the process working? A team can be happy and still miss the deadline if nobody manages the **means**.',
        '**Leadership** is about whether people want to and can do the work. A perfect plan fails if the team doesn’t trust it. A quick way to remember it: manage things, lead people.'],
    8: ['Anyone can show leadership, for example by helping a stuck teammate or raising a concern, without any title. **Management** is different, because someone has to be assigned tasks like budgets and reporting, and an organization can give them to one person or share them out.',
        '**Accountability** means that when something goes wrong, there is a known person who has to answer for it. Centralizing it makes things clear: everyone knows who decides and who to escalate to.',
        'Being named PM isn’t enough, since you need the right to spend money and pull people from other departments. The **project charter** is the document that formally gives that right, so nobody can later ask who authorized it.'],
    9: ['Here the PM’s tasks are split up, such as one person tracking budget and another handling risks, while the rest of the team builds. It suits teams that are experienced enough not to need one central decision-maker.',
        'To **self-organize** means nobody assigns the work: the team decides among themselves who does what and how. It depends on a mature team, because it collapses if nobody takes responsibility.',
        'A **facilitator** doesn’t give orders. They run the conversation and remove obstacles, as a Scrum master does. **The role can shift** because it isn’t a rank, so whoever suits the current stage can take it on.'],
    11: ['These come first because missing them causes problems no process can fix. If people disagree on the goal or on who owns what, you get duplicate work and gaps. Agreeing on **team operations** (how meetings, decisions and communication work) avoids daily friction.',
         '**Guidance** isn’t only top-down. The PM keeps the whole team heading the same way, while the most experienced person on a task may guide others on it, such as the database expert advising on queries.',
         '**Growth** needs honesty in both directions. Naming what works keeps it going, and naming what doesn’t gives the team something specific to improve. That’s what a retrospective does.'],
    12: ['Put two teams in one company and they can still feel completely different, in how they argue, how fast they reply and whether they admit mistakes. A team’s **own culture** always forms, so the only question is whether anyone shapes it.',
         'A culture built **deliberately** starts with agreed rules: “we review every pull request within a day.” If nobody agrees anything, culture forms **informally** from behavior, for example if the PM ignores late work, the team learns lateness is fine.',
         'A team can’t ignore its company’s rules, so a bank’s project team still follows the bank’s compliance culture. Inside those limits, it can still set its own habits. Think of a family living under a country’s laws.'],
    13: ['People hide bad news if speaking up gets them punished, and hidden problems are the expensive ones. A **safe, respectful, nonjudgmental** environment makes it normal to say “I’m stuck” early, and the PM is the one who sets that tone.',
         'Teams copy what you do, not what you say. A PM who admits a mistake in a meeting teaches more than any poster about honesty. **Courage** matters most here, since it’s what gets problems raised before they grow.',
         'Recognition tells people that their effort was noticed, which is a big part of why they keep giving it. **Celebrating success** can be small, like thanking someone in a review, and works best when it’s specific.'],
    14: ['A team can’t work together toward something it pictures differently. **Open communication** surfaces problems, **shared understanding** means everyone pictures the same goal, and **shared ownership** means nobody says “that’s not my part” when something breaks.',
         '**Trust** lets people ask for help without fear, and **collaboration** is what trust makes possible. Without them, people protect their own piece of the work and the pieces don’t fit together.',
         'Projects never go as planned. **Adaptability** is changing course when a requirement changes, and **resilience** is recovering after a real setback, such as a failed release.',
         '**Empowerment** means people can decide without waiting for approval, which speeds everything up. **Recognition** makes them want to keep doing it. Empowerment without recognition burns people out.'],
    17: ['No style is always right, so the PM picks one that suits the situation. **Autocratic** can work in a crisis when a fast decision is needed, while **servant** leadership suits a skilled team that only needs obstacles removed. Using one fixed style is the mistake.',
         'Each variable changes how much direction people need. A team new to this type of project needs more guidance. A mature team would resent it. Strict **governance** limits what you can leave to the team, and a **distributed** team needs more deliberate communication because nobody overhears anything.'],
    19: ['This domain answers three questions: how do we build it (**development approach**), how often do we deliver (**cadence**), and what stages do we go through (**life-cycle phases**)? The answers have to fit together.',
         'The approach has to match what you’re building: you can’t construct a bridge in two-week releases. The phases then need to fit the rhythm you promised, so that value reaches people when expected. That’s what **consistent with the deliverables** means.'],
    20: ['The test is in two words: **unique** (specific to this work) and **verifiable** (someone can check it exists and is correct). “A signed test report” is a deliverable. “Better teamwork” isn’t, since you can’t verify it.',
         'This is the “how do we build it?” choice. **Predictive** plans everything first, while **adaptive** learns as it goes. **Iterative** and **incremental** are ways of working within those. Chapter 4 compares them in detail.',
         '**Cadence** is a rhythm, like a heartbeat. When everyone knows a demo happens every second Thursday, they plan around it without needing extra coordination.',
         'A **phase** is a chunk of work that ends with something to show, such as “design” ending in approved designs. The **life cycle** is all the phases in order, so a phase is a chapter and the life cycle is the book.'],
    21: ['Everything flows from what you’re making. Software can be released in pieces, but half a bridge is useless. So the **type of deliverable** limits your options before anyone chooses a method.',
         'Once the method is chosen, it affects how often you can ship. A physical product may ship once, while a website can ship weekly. Promises to the customer about **cadence** have to respect that.',
         'This is the last link in the chain: deliverable → approach and cadence → **life cycle**. If you plan monthly releases, your phases have to repeat monthly. A single long “build” phase wouldn’t fit.',
         '“When and how often does the customer receive something?” is all this means. Don’t confuse it with **cadence** in general, which covers any rhythm of activity, such as weekly meetings. Delivery cadence is specifically about **deliveries**.',
         'Pick by what the customer needs. A **single** delivery fits a one-off building, **multiple** fit a system handed over in stages, and **periodic** fit something like monthly payroll updates. Earlier deliveries mean earlier value and earlier feedback.'],
    22: ['Think of apps that update without you noticing. **Continuous delivery** is only realistic for digital products, because you can’t ship a physical product’s improvements daily.',
         'Why small batches? A small change is easy to test and easy to undo if it breaks something. **Automation** makes the frequent releasing cheap, because no person has to repeat the build and test steps each time.',
         'A project mindset asks “did we finish?”, while a **product** mindset asks “is it still delivering value?”. With continuous delivery, the team can react to what the market wants this month, not what was planned last year.',
         'You’ll see this practice again under **DevOps** in Chapter 4, where development and operations work as one team. #noprojects is the extreme view that you fund ongoing products rather than one-off projects.'],
    23: ['It’s the answer to “what way of working will we use to produce this?”. It isn’t the tools or the team. Waterfall and agile are examples of a **development approach**.',
         'If an exam or company uses an unfamiliar name, look at how the work is done, not the label. One industry’s “stage-gate” may be another’s “waterfall”. **Different industries** use different terms, but the idea is the same.',
         'The three common ones are **predictive, hybrid and adaptive**, a **spectrum** rather than three boxes: the further toward adaptive, the more iterative and incremental the work.'],
    24: ['**Predictive** bets that you can know what’s needed before you start, such as a building with approved blueprints. It works when requirements won’t move much. It’s also called **waterfall** because each stage flows into the next.',
         'The second reason is about stakes, not certainty. When a mistake is very costly, like a hospital information system, you want formal checks before spending the next large sum. Heavy reviews would slow a small app down, but here they’re protection.',
         'Knowing these early is what makes predictive possible, because you can commit to a price and date up front. If they keep changing, those commitments keep breaking, and that’s the sign you chose the wrong approach.',
         'It tries to remove uncertainty before building starts, so most **planning** happens **up front**. The cost is that late changes are expensive, as you’d be undoing finished work.'],
    25: ['**Hybrid** is about mixing, not compromising. You use the predictive way where things are stable and the adaptive way where they aren’t, in the same project.',
         'It’s useful when you’re sure about some parts and not others. A payment system’s rules may be fixed by law, while its user interface needs trying out with real users. Using one approach for both would be wrong for one of them.',
         'Pieces that can be built separately can be run differently. **Modularized** means the parts connect but don’t depend on each other’s way of working, like a fixed back-end integration planned predictively with a user-facing app built in sprints.'],
    27: ['**Adaptive** assumes that you can’t know everything up front, so it doesn’t pretend to. It suits new products, where users themselves don’t yet know what they want until they try something.',
         'Not knowing the details doesn’t mean having no direction. The **clear vision** is the fixed point, like “students can register online in under a minute”. The features that achieve it can change as you learn from user feedback.',
         'Adaptive work repeats (**iterative**) and grows (**incremental**). The further toward the adaptive end, the shorter each cycle gets, since shorter cycles mean faster correction when you’re wrong.'],
    28: ['A framework is a set of steps, while **agility** is an attitude of responding to change. A team can follow every Scrum ritual and still not be agile. The **1–2 week iterations** ending in a demo force real progress to be shown often.',
         'People commit more to plans they helped make, and they know the real effort better than anyone else. That’s why the **team plans** each iteration in agile, rather than receiving a plan from above.',
         'Because the backlog is **prioritized**, the most valuable work comes first and whatever doesn’t fit is the least important. If time runs out, you lose the least. The team sets the amount it takes on, which makes its promise realistic.'],
    29: ['The common thread is how much you can know in advance and how cheaply you can change. New ideas, unclear requirements and easy changes all favor learning as you go, like a mobile app. Rule of thumb: high **innovation**, uncertain **requirements**, unstable **scope**, easy **change** and frequent **delivery options** push toward adaptive.',
         'Strict **safety requirements** and **regulations** push toward predictive (they need documented, controlled phases).'],
    30: ['The product isn’t the only factor. Even for the same product, the approach can change depending on who’s involved, how soon something is needed and how the money arrives. These are the three project variables to name: **stakeholders, schedule constraints and funding availability**.',
         'Adaptive only works if someone is available to answer questions and give feedback every week. A busy customer who only wants to see the final result is a warning sign. A **product owner** fills this role by deciding what comes next.',
         'Imagine needing to show something at a trade fair in six weeks while the full product takes a year. Delivering something usable **early** only works when the work is split into cycles, which a single big delivery can’t give you.',
         'If next quarter’s money isn’t certain, a big plan risks being stopped halfway, with nothing usable. Working in cycles means there’s always something finished, so you lose less if **funding** stops…',
         '…and there’s a further benefit: an **MVP** is the smallest version worth releasing. Releasing it tests the idea cheaply, and if the market responds well, that success can help secure more funding.'],
    31: ['Even if the product suits an approach, the organization has to be able to run it. Skills, habits and how the company is arranged can all make an approach fail, so **structure, culture, capability, team size and location** all count.',
         'A strongly hierarchical, documentation-heavy culture struggles with adaptive work; a small co-located team adopts it easily; large or distributed teams need more structure.'],
    32: ['A **phase gate** stops a project from rolling on by momentum. Before spending on the next phase, someone asks “did we actually get what this phase was meant to produce?”. If not, you fix it or stop, which is cheaper than finding out later.',
         '**Exit criteria** have to be checkable, or the gate means nothing. “Design is mostly done” can’t be verified, while “all designs are approved and 95% of test cases pass” can, and that’s why they’re tied to **acceptance criteria** or contract terms.',
         'The diagrams: a **predictive** life cycle (feasibility → design → build → test → deploy → close, one phase after another) and an **incremental** one (plan → design → build repeated, each round delivering an increment).'],
    36: ['An **estimate** is an informed number, not a promise or a guess, and it should come with a basis. “About 40 days, based on two similar projects” can be challenged and improved, while “it’ll take a while” can’t.',
         '**Accuracy** asks one question: was it right? Estimate $100K, actual $102K → accurate, because the estimate landed close to reality, however rough it was.',
         '**Precision** = **exactness**: how detailed and consistent the estimate is. A precise estimate (every cost broken down) can still be **inaccurate** if the project ends up costing much more. Accurate = hits the target; precise = tightly grouped.'],
    37: ['**Proactively** is the key word. Planning means deciding before problems force you to, as it’s far cheaper to choose an approach now than to change course mid-build.',
         'It sounds backwards to plan before approval, but someone must estimate cost and benefit so leaders can decide whether to authorize it at all. That’s why **high-level planning** comes first, in the business case.',
         '**Progressively elaborates** means adding detail as you learn, rather than guessing everything on day one. A vision statement becomes a charter, then a detailed plan, each more specific because more is now known.',
         'A project can be profitable and still cause harm, like a product that’s hard to dispose of. Considering **social and environmental** impact early is cheaper than fixing it later, and customers and regulators increasingly expect it.'],
    38: ['You can’t plan a route without knowing the destination. The **business case** says why we’re doing this, requirements say what people need, and scope says what’s included. Skipping these means planning the wrong thing very well.',
         'Ask: “what will the finished thing have?” For a banking app, login, transfers and statements are **product scope**. It describes the result, and says nothing about how the team gets there.',
         '**Project scope**: the **work** performed to deliver it (what the team must do). Don’t swap them; near-identical terms are the instructor’s favourite trap.'],
    39: ['If you know what you need, you can break it down from the top. The **WBS** is like an outline: a building becomes floors, floors become rooms, rooms become tasks. Small pieces are what you can estimate and assign.',
         'Here the breakdown happens gradually. An **epic** like “online payments” is split into features, then **user stories** like “as a customer, I can save my card”. Only the near-term items get broken down, as later ones may change.',
         'If something might fail, you want to find out early and cheaply. Doing the **risky or novel** work first means that if the new technology won’t work, you learn it before you’ve spent most of the budget.',
         'Deciding too early means deciding with less information. **Last responsible moment** means waiting until just before further delay would start costing more, like booking a venue only once the date is certain, but before they sell out.'],
    40: ['You need these numbers to answer “can we afford this, and when will it finish?”. Each one feeds something else: **effort** and people give duration, and duration and rates give **cost**.',
         '**Quantitative** means a number you can use and compare, such as 40 days or $5,000. Saying “it’s a big task” doesn’t let anyone plan.',
         'An early estimate is rough because you know little, so expecting it to hold is unrealistic. **Estimates change** as you learn, and updating them is good practice, not failure (the “cone of uncertainty” in Chapter 6).'],
    41: ['A **schedule** is called a **model** because it’s a simulation of how the work should unfold. Change one duration and you can see what moves. It isn’t just a list of dates.',
         'The schedule isn’t always a fixed sequence of dates. **Predictive** fixes the plan up front, while **adaptive** plans in short cycles and adjusts. Which one you use follows from your development approach.',
         'Each step needs the one before. You can’t order activities you haven’t listed, you can’t estimate resources before knowing what they do, and you can’t allocate people without estimates. Decompose → sequence → estimate → allocate → adjust.'],
    42: ['A contract or physical reality forces this order, as you can’t test code before it’s written. **Mandatory** is what you check first when trying to shorten a schedule, because this kind **cannot** be rearranged.',
         'This is the team’s choice, like “finish the design review before coding”. It’s a good habit but not a law, so **discretionary** links are what you relax when fast-tracking. Doing so adds risk.',
         'You’re waiting on someone outside the project, like a government permit or a vendor’s delivery. **External** links are out of your control, so all you can do is plan around them and monitor them.',
         'Both activities belong to your project, such as “finish the API before the app connects to it”. Since it’s all in the team’s hands, an **internal** link can often be reorganized.'],
    43: ['Instead of scheduling every task for a year, you plan in layers: rough for later, detailed for soon. **Incremental planning** avoids carefully scheduling work that will have changed by the time you reach it.',
         'A **release plan** is a commitment about what customers will get and roughly when, like “Release 1: login and profiles”. It gives stakeholders something to plan around without detailing every task.',
         'Iterations break a release into short steps, so there’s a working check on progress every few weeks, well before the release date. Each is a chance to correct course.',
         'Value doesn’t just mean new features. An iteration spent proving a risky technology works, or running an experiment, protects the project. So **risk reduction** counts too, even with nothing new on the screen.',
         'Detailed plans for next year are wasted effort, as feedback from this release will change them. Keeping **future releases** high level saves that work and keeps the team free to act on what it learns.'],
    45: ['A **timebox** flips the usual approach. Normally scope is fixed and time slips. Here time is fixed and scope flexes, so whatever’s finished at the end is delivered. A sprint is the common example.',
         'A **prioritized backlog** ensures the team takes the most valuable items first. If something doesn’t fit, it’s the least important thing that waits.',
         'People doing the work know best how long it takes, and they commit more firmly to their own estimates. **Self-managing** also saves everyone from waiting for instructions.',
         'A **demonstration** makes progress impossible to fake. “90% done” can mean anything, while working software in front of stakeholders can’t, and they often spot misunderstandings right away.',
         'This is where learning turns into action: what stakeholders just saw can change what’s most valuable next. **Reprioritizing** every timebox is why this approach handles change well.'],
    46: ['A **budget** isn’t an arbitrary figure handed down. It starts from what the work is estimated to cost, and it has to be agreed on, because those people will be held to it.',
         '**Aggregate** just means adding up. Task costs roll up into work packages and then the whole project, and the total becomes the **cost baseline** you measure against.',
         'Knowing the total isn’t enough, because you also need to know when the cash is needed. **Time-phased** means spread across the schedule: little in month one, then a big spike when equipment is purchased.',
         'Finance often releases money by quarter or year. **Balancing the funds** means checking that the work you’ve scheduled for a period doesn’t cost more than that period’s approved money.',
         'If only $50K is available in Q1 but the plan needs $80K there, something has to move. **Rescheduling** shifts work to Q2, which may delay the project. That’s why funding limits affect the schedule as well as the money.'],
    47: ['Every project has things that can go wrong and cost money. A budget with no **contingency reserve** assumes nothing will, which isn’t realistic, and the first surprise puts you over budget.',
         'You know this risk exists, you just don’t know whether it will happen, like a key supplier being late. Because it’s identified, the **PM** can use this money without going up the chain. Think “known unknowns”.',
         'This is for things nobody saw coming, like a sudden new regulation. Since it can’t be tied to a planned risk, **upper management** controls it, and the PM has to ask for it. Think “unknown unknowns”.',
         'Who holds the money depends on how much control the organization wants, so the **PMO** or sponsor may hold it. What counts for exams is that it sits outside the PM’s normal control.'],
    49: ['If it isn’t a person but the project needs it, count it here. **Not a person** covers more than you’d guess, including things you can’t touch.',
         'Software teams often forget this applies to them. **Testing environments** and licenses cost money and take time to set up, and running out of them can stop work just as a missing machine would.',
         'For physical items, knowing how many isn’t enough. **Supply chain** and logistics ask how they get here, who delivers them and where they’re stored, since a late shipment stops everyone waiting on it.',
         'If you need steel or cranes, you must buy or rent them, and that takes contracts and time. So **procurement** becomes a major part of planning on such projects.',
         '**Lead time** is the wait between ordering and receiving. If equipment takes 12 weeks, ordering late delays everything. Storage and disposal matter too, because materials need somewhere to go.'],
    50: ['You can buy things at any point, but contracts take time and involve legal rules, so a late start can hold everyone up. Thinking ahead about **procurement** avoids surprises.',
         'You need to know roughly what you’re building before you can decide which parts to buy. So the **make-or-buy analysis** starts once the high-level scope is clear, and before detailed planning.',
         'Do we build the login system or buy one? Building needs people and time, while buying needs money and a contract. So this choice changes who is on the team and how long things take, which is why it affects the schedule.'],
    51: ['Plans say what should happen, delivery is what did happen, and measurement compares the two. Without that comparison, you can’t tell if you’re on track until it’s too late.',
         '**Metrics** are the connection: the same measure you plan with (say, 20 items per sprint) is what you check during delivery.',
         'A number alone doesn’t tell you whether to act. **Thresholds** give it meaning: agreeing in advance that “5% over budget is a warning, 10% is unacceptable” means nobody argues later about whether there’s a problem.',
         'Measuring takes time away from doing the work. **“Only measure what matters”** means that if a number wouldn’t change any decision, you should stop collecting it.'],
    54: ['These are what a seller reads before replying. Clear **bid documents** mean offers you can compare fairly. Vague ones get you prices for different things.',
         'If one vendor gets extra details, the process isn’t fair and the bids can’t be compared. A **bidder conference** gives every seller the same answers at the same time, before they write their offers.',
         'If it can be written down and handed to someone, it’s **explicit**, like a setup guide. It’s easy to store and share, which also means it survives when people leave.',
         '**Tacit knowledge**: personal and hard to articulate (beliefs, experience, insights), e.g. a senior developer’s instinct for which module will break. It is shared through mentoring, pairing and conversation.'],
    55: ['Between planning and delivery comes the daily running of a project. This domain is about keeping work moving and removing distractions so that people can **focus**.',
         'New requests keep arriving during a project. Without somewhere to put them, people get pulled in every direction. Managing the **flow of work** means deciding what gets done now and what waits.',
         'A missing part or a slow contract can stall work just as surely as a software bug. So **procurement** and logistics aren’t side jobs here, and watching for changes means catching problems before they spread.',
         'What one person learns is lost if it stays with them. **Knowledge transfer** means that when someone solves a hard problem or leaves, the team keeps what they learned.'],
    56: ['A process that worked in month one may not suit month six, once the team or the work has changed. That’s why it isn’t set once and forgotten: you **review** it **periodically**.',
         'For example, review **task boards** for **bottlenecks** (work piling up in one column), whether work flows at the expected rate, and **impediments** blocking progress.'],
    57: ['A process should fit the project, not the other way around. **Process tailoring** means removing steps that add nothing here and adding ones this project needs.',
         'Failure costs more on some projects. A medical device needs heavy documentation because errors can hurt people, while a small internal tool doesn’t. So how **critical** a project is matters as much as its size.',
         'These are all ways of asking “is this effort worth it?”. **Lean** removes steps that add no value, **retrospectives** find what to change, and the funding question stops you polishing something that’s already good enough.'],
    58: ['**Waste** is any effort that adds no value, such as three approvals when one would do. Reviewing processes finds these, and it’s often the cheapest way to speed up a project.',
         'Every hour spent proving you followed the process is an hour not spent building. Checking **conformance** has a real cost, so more checking isn’t automatically better.',
         'Too little review and you drift out of compliance. Too much and nothing gets built. **Just enough** means the amount that gets the benefit and satisfies governance, which is the same idea as tailoring.'],
    59: ['A **constraint** is a limit you can’t simply wish away. You’re working within them from the start, so a PM who doesn’t know what’s truly fixed can’t make good trade-offs.',
         'Knowing which of these is fixed tells you where you have room. If the launch date is set by law, then scope or budget must flex instead. A **fixed delivery date** and a **predetermined budget** are the usual examples.',
         'A budget approved in January may be cut in June. So balancing **constraints** isn’t a one-time decision, and you need to keep checking what’s still fixed.',
         'New work needs more time and money, unless something else is removed. A **new stakeholder requirement** is a good test of whether a PM can explain the cost of saying yes.',
         '…and a **budget cut** may relax a quality requirement or reduce scope. They are interrelated: change one, and the others move.'],
    60: ['Nothing stays balanced for long, since a new risk or a late supplier shifts things again. Balancing is **ongoing** work for the whole project, not a task done once in planning.',
         'Don’t just bring problems to the sponsor, bring choices. “We can cut feature X or slip two weeks; here’s what each costs” lets them decide quickly. Presenting **alternatives and their implications** does that.',
         'Not every trade-off needs a meeting with the sponsor. If it doesn’t affect what was promised, like swapping two tasks, the **team’s authority** covers it, and that keeps things moving.'],
    61: ['Few projects can do everything themselves. As soon as you buy, rent or hire from outside, legal commitments are involved, which is why **contracting** deserves its own attention.',
         'Don’t just picture buying machines: hiring a consultant or a software company is also procurement. **Materials, labor and services** are all covered by the same rules.',
         'A contract legally binds the organization, so the right to sign one is tightly limited. A PM who agrees terms with a vendor informally can create real legal problems, because they have **no contracting authority**.',
         'The PM knows what’s needed, and the **contracting officer** knows the law. They rely on each other: one defines the work, and the other makes the agreement valid and safe.'],
    62: ['Spending the organization’s money without controls invites fraud and legal disputes. **Rigorous procurement policies** spell out who may sign what, and up to what amount, so that no one is left guessing.',
         'A lawyer can’t judge whether a technical requirement is right, and an engineer can’t judge a contract clause, so they write these together. The **SOW** matters most: if it’s vague, you get arguments about what was promised.'],
    63: ['Sellers need a fair chance and buyers need comparable offers. So there’s a fixed order: publish what you want, answer questions equally, then choose. Skipping steps invites disputes about the **bid process**.',
         'Use an **RFI** when you don’t yet know what’s possible. You’re researching rather than buying, so it commits you to nothing.',
         '**RFP** (request for proposal): “how would you build it?”, used once the type of solution is chosen, to invite vendors to propose and bid.',
         '**RFQ** (request for quote): “what’s your price?”, used for a known, specified item (500 laptops with exact specs).'],
    67: ['“Done” means different things to different people. A developer may mean “it runs on my machine”, while a customer means “I can use it”. The **definition of done** removes the argument by listing what has to be true.',
         '**Quality** is measured against what was required, not how impressive something is. A simple tool that does exactly what was asked is high quality, while a beautiful one that misses a requirement isn’t.',
         '**Cost of quality (COQ)**: all costs over the product’s life from **prevention** (training, standards), **appraisal** (testing, inspection) and **failure** (rework, warranty, lost customers). Chapter 7 develops it.'],
    68: ['If you ship in pieces, users benefit from the first piece right away, and it can start earning or saving money. With a single delivery at the end, the value only begins once **the project is over**.',
         'The project team may have disbanded long ago while the product is still earning. So you can’t judge a project’s value on its closing day, and benefits may be measured for years **after the project ends**.',
         'A **business case** answers “why is this worth doing?”, and it’s what you check later to see whether the value arrived. An adaptive project may use a lighter version, because the details are expected to change.'],
    69: ['Delivering every requested feature can still fail. A system with all its functions but which crashes under load isn’t a success, so **more than scope** has to be considered.',
         '**Scope** is the list of things the customer gets. Ticking every item off the list tells you nothing about how well each one works.',
         'Quality says **how well**: the **performance levels** that must be met (response time, reliability, accuracy).',
         'A quality requirement only helps if the team can see it, so it needs writing down somewhere they use. Putting “loads in under 2 seconds” in the **definition of done** means it’s checked each time.'],
    70: ['Quality work is often paid for before your project starts. Standards and quality procedures already exist, which means the **sponsoring organization** absorbs much of this cost.',
         'Company-wide rules, such as “all code needs review”, express its **quality policy**. Your project inherits them, and you normally can’t opt out.',
         'Your project benefits from training and **audits** without paying for them directly. That’s why the true cost of quality may be hidden from the project’s budget.',
         'Perfect quality would cost a fortune and take forever, while no quality causes failure. The right level is where further spending is no longer worth it, which is why projects **balance** quality against cost.',
         'A bug found in review costs little, while one found by customers can cost far more. The **cost of quality** idea is that money spent on prevention usually saves more than it costs.'],
    71: ['Doing everything right doesn’t guarantee success, because projects create something new. So **suboptimal outcomes** aren’t proof of bad management, and the question is whether you limited the damage.',
         'Here failure is expected and paid for, since the organization knows it’s buying a chance of a breakthrough. A drug company accepts many failures to find one medicine, so a **fully experimental** project needs judging differently.',
         'A project can be delivered perfectly and still be too late. If a competitor launched first, the **market opportunity** may be gone. This is one more reason why speed of delivery has value.',
         'You can reduce the chance and the cost of failure, but not remove them. **Good project management** spots trouble early and limits the damage, which is why exam answers saying it “eliminates” failure are wrong.'],
    74: ['“Progress is good” isn’t a **metric**. It becomes one when you state what you’re measuring and how: “percent of planned work finished”. That lets two people get the same answer.',
         'You can’t say you’re behind without something to compare with. The **baseline** is the agreed plan, and it’s approved so that it can’t quietly be changed to make results look better.',
         'A **dashboard** gives a quick view. Rather than reading a long report, someone can see in a glance what’s off track and then ask about it.'],
    75: ['Measuring tells you whether reality matches the plan while there’s still time to act. You can’t correct a budget overrun that you only find out about at the end.',
         'Numbers keep things honest. **Accountability** is easier to show with a record of what was promised and delivered, and stakeholders get facts rather than reassurance.',
         'A project can be on time and still heading for no benefit. Data also makes hard discussions easier: “we’ll miss this date, so which feature do we drop?” is better than opinions, and it helps **focus conversations**.',
         'It’s far better to learn that a deliverable will fail before handing it over. Testing against **customer acceptance criteria** along the way avoids a nasty surprise at sign-off.'],
    76: ['A report nobody acts on is wasted work. The **value** comes when someone asks “this number looks bad, so what do we do?”. If no decision comes out of it, the measure isn’t helping.',
         'Those conversations also expose bad measures. If people keep ignoring a number, it may be the wrong thing to track. So talking about data helps you measure the **right things**.',
         'Slow decline is the hard kind to notice, since each week looks only slightly worse. Tracking over time reveals the trend, so you can act before performance **deteriorates** badly.'],
    77: ['A **KPI** is one of the few measures that really shows whether things are going well. **Leading** indicators are the valuable kind because they warn you early, like dark clouds before rain, while you can still act.',
         'Why do these predict trouble? Lots of scope change requests suggest that requirements weren’t understood, so delays are likely to follow. An overloaded team will soon start missing dates, which is why **team workload** is worth watching now.',
         '**Lagging** indicators report what already happened, like a final exam grade. They’re accurate and easy to measure, but it’s too late to change that result. You can only learn from them.',
         'Cost variance can only be calculated after the money is spent, and satisfaction only after people use the product. So you need both: leading to steer and lagging to confirm. Leading looks ahead; lagging looks back.'],
    78: ['Measurement isn’t free, since every hour spent collecting data is an hour not spent building. So ask of each measure “what would we do differently if this changed?”, and if nothing, drop it.',
         'Effective metrics are **SMART**: Specific, **Meaningful**, Achievable, Relevant, Timely. Note that in this slide the M is “meaningful”, not the usual “measurable”: a classic trap.'],
    79: ['There’s no universal set. A safety-critical system tracks defects closely, while a marketing campaign watches customer response. So start from what success means for this project and **environment**.',
         'The categories ask different questions: is it good (**deliverable**), is it flowing (**delivery**), are we on plan (**baseline**), are we using things well (**resources**), is it worth it (**business value**), are people happy (**stakeholders**), where will we end up (**forecasts**)? Slides 80–81 give examples of each.'],
    80: ['These judge the thing being built. A high count of **defects** warns of trouble even when the schedule looks fine, and **technical performance** checks things like speed against the target.',
         '**Delivery**: work in progress, **lead time** (from identifying the need to delivering the result: the customer’s whole wait), **cycle time** (from starting the work to finishing it), process efficiency.',
         'These compare reality to the approved plan. **Schedule variance** is the gap: a negative number means you’re behind, which tells you how far you’ve drifted and that you may need to act.'],
    81: ['Comparing plan and actual reveals problems early. Using far more of a **resource** than planned hints that the estimate was wrong, and that the cost will overrun too.',
         'These ask “is this worth the money?” in different ways. **ROI** compares gain to cost, while **NPV** also accounts for money later being worth less than money today. They’re what a sponsor cares about.',
         'Unhappy people are an early warning sign. **NPS** asks how likely customers are to recommend you, while morale and **turnover** show the team’s health. Losing people is expensive and a sign something’s wrong.',
         '**Forecasts**: estimate to complete (ETC: what is left), **estimate at completion (EAC**: the expected total), variance at completion (VAC = budget − EAC).'],
    89: ['**Uncertainty** is the umbrella term, meaning you simply don’t know something, like whether a new technology will work. The other terms on this slide are different kinds of not knowing.',
         'The key feature of **ambiguity** is unclarity rather than lack of data. Several answers seem possible and you can’t tell which is right, like not knowing which of **multiple options** fixes a bug.',
         'Many parts affecting one another make results hard to predict. **Complexity** isn’t the same as being difficult: a hard math problem can be solved, while a system of people and interacting parts can surprise you.',
         'Think of how **volatility** works in a market: a steel price jumping 30% in a month. You may understand it well, and it can still change without warning.',
         'A **risk** is specific: “the vendor may deliver late”. It may happen or not. Exam trap: the definition includes **positive** effects, so a risk isn’t always bad.'],
    90: ['This slide steps back. Whenever you can’t say for sure what will happen or what’s true, that’s **uncertainty**, and the next three terms describe why.',
         'Compare them by what’s unknown. **Risk**: a specific future event may happen. **Ambiguity**: you aren’t sure what the current situation means. **Complexity**: too many interacting parts to predict. Each one needs a different response, which is the reason to separate them.'],
    91: ['Many surprises come from outside the project. You can’t respond to what you never looked at, so scanning the **larger environment** comes before responding.',
         'Use it as a checklist when asked where uncertainty comes from. **Economic**: costs change. **Technical**: new technology may not work. **Legal**: the rules may change. Each is something the team doesn’t control but must plan for.',
         '**Political** influences, inside or outside the organization. (A PESTLE-style checklist.)'],
    92: ['Two people can read the same sentence differently and both feel sure. **Conceptual ambiguity** is fixed by defining terms: agree on what “done” or “on track” means before relying on the word. The slide’s schedule sentence shows how easily it happens.',
         'Here the words are clear, but you can’t tell which path will turn out best. With **situational ambiguity**, the way forward is trying options on a small scale or making them comparable, rather than arguing.'],
    93: ['This isn’t about the amount of work. A huge but predictable job can be merely large. **Complexity** comes from people and systems reacting in ways you can’t fully foresee.',
         '**Interconnected** is the key word: change one thing and others respond, and those responses cause more change. Think of a new policy that makes one department’s work easier and creates a backlog in another.'],
    94: ['The whole can behave differently from its parts. Each person can act sensibly and still produce a traffic jam. That’s what **unintended outcomes** means, and it’s why testing parts alone doesn’t guarantee the whole works.',
         'This changes how you should act. Since you **can’t predict** results, a detailed long-term plan offers false confidence. Instead, try small steps, watch what happens and adjust.',
         'Each family tackles it from another angle: **systems-based** changes the system itself, **reframing** changes how you look at it, and **process-based** changes how you work. Next three slides.'],
    95: ['If everything is connected, every change affects everything. **Decoupling** cuts those links so you can work on one piece safely. Software modules with clear interfaces are the familiar example, as you can change one without breaking the rest.',
         'When you can’t test the real thing, learn from something similar. A **simulation** doesn’t have to use the same industry: an airport can learn from **shopping malls** how customers spend, at low cost and before anything’s built.'],
    96: ['One person only sees part of a complex system. **Diversity** brings in other views to uncover what you’d miss. Starting broad and narrowing later keeps you from settling too soon on the first idea. Delphi-like processes then narrow things down.',
         'Relying only on past data misses what’s coming, and relying only on forecasts ignores what really happened. **Balance** uses both, and the slide’s other point is like a hedge: elements that **counteract** each other limit the total damage.'],
    97: ['If you can’t predict the result, make small changes and watch. **Iterating** turns a big unknown into many small experiments, and each one tells you what to try next.',
         'Assumptions are where complex projects go wrong. Asking stakeholders regularly replaces guesses with facts. **Engaging** them also means they’re less likely to be surprised, or to resist the final result.',
         '**Fail safe**: for critical elements, build in **redundancy** or **graceful degradation** so a component failure doesn’t take the whole system down (a backup server).'],
    98: ['Compare it with ambiguity. **Volatility** isn’t about being unclear: you may understand a market well, and prices can still jump next week.',
         'It often comes from **fluctuations** in available skills or materials (a key specialist leaves; chip prices spike).',
         'Sudden shortages and price jumps show up directly as more money or more waiting. So **cost and schedule** are where you plan protection, which is what the next slide covers.'],
    99: ['You can’t stop the changes, so prepare for them with two tools: have other options ready and keep something in reserve. That’s why **alternatives analysis** and **reserves** go together.',
         'If your one specialist leaves, what then? **Alternatives analysis** answers before it happens: train someone else, reorder the work, or **outsource**. Having options means a shock doesn’t stop you.',
         'Prices may rise no matter what you do, so a **cost reserve** absorbs the jump without needing a new approval. Without it, every price change would become a crisis.'],
    100: ['Uncertainty is the broad state of not knowing. A **risk** is one specific thing you can name, assess and plan a response to, and that’s why it’s manageable.',
          'Two parts make up the definition: it might not happen (uncertain), and if it does, an objective is affected. If it has already happened, it’s no longer a risk but an issue. Remember the effect can be **positive**.',
          'Separate words help because you act in opposite ways: you try to stop **threats** from happening and try to make **opportunities** happen. Exam questions often turn on that difference.',
          'Having never done this exact thing before means you can’t know everything in advance. So “no risks” in a plan means that nobody looked, and every project has them because each is **unique**.'],
    101: ['Separate risks can each look fine while the project as a whole is shaky. **Overall project risk** looks at the total picture, and a very complex, unclear, fast-changing project is risky even if no single item stands out.',
          'Same toolkit, larger scale. Rather than buying insurance for a single event, you might split a whole project into phases, or partner with another firm to share the **project as a whole**’s exposure.',
          'If overall risk is too high, the organization may **cancel** the project: a legitimate outcome, not a failure of risk management.'],
    102: ['A **threat** is still only a possibility. That’s why you have choices about it, including accepting it or preventing it, which you wouldn’t have once it has happened.',
          '**Avoid**: eliminate the threat or protect the project from it (change the plan, drop the risky feature).',
          '**Escalate**: the threat is outside the PM’s authority or scope, so pass it to the right level (program, portfolio, sponsor).',
          '**Transfer**: shift ownership and impact to a third party (insurance, a fixed-price contract).',
          '**Mitigate**: reduce the probability or impact (extra testing, a prototype).',
          '**Accept**: acknowledge it without action, perhaps with a contingency reserve.'],
    104: ['Same event logic as a threat, but here you want it to happen. Since an **opportunity** can be missed, ignoring one has a cost too, so it deserves as much attention as a threat.',
          'Finishing early sounds like simple luck, but a PM should plan for it. Under a time-and-materials contract, you pay for hours worked, so an early finish **saves money** directly, which can be reused elsewhere.',
          '**Exploit**: act to make sure the opportunity **happens** (raise its probability to 100%).',
          '**Share**: give part of the ownership to a third party better able to capture the benefit (a partnership).',
          'The difference from exploit is the guarantee. **Enhance** improves the odds or the size, such as adding staff to raise the chance of finishing early, without promising it. **Escalate** and **accept** apply to opportunities too.'],
    105: ['Reserves are a planned cushion, not padding. Being explicit means people can see what’s there for risk and track how it’s used, instead of hiding extra days in every task.',
          'You’ve named the risk and estimated the possible hit, so this is planned money. Since it’s tied to **identified** risks, it’s normally part of the cost baseline and the PM can use it.',
          'Nobody can name these events yet, so you can’t assign the money to specific risks. **Management reserve** stays outside the baseline, and using it takes approval (see slide 47).'],
    106: ['Risks change as a project moves, so a list written once goes stale. A regular **cadence** catches new ones, and more varied people mean more chance someone spots what others can’t see.',
          'Nobody has to run a separate risk meeting for this. **Daily stand-ups** already ask what’s blocking people, which is where threats first appear, while they’re still small.',
          'A small delay today can become a missed milestone if it continues. A **blocker** is a threat in its early form, so treat a repeat report as a warning, not just an update.',
          'Good news deserves a follow-up too. If a team finds a faster method, could another team use it? A **breakthrough** is an opportunity only if someone notices and acts on it.',
          'People react differently to something real than to a description. Showing a working piece early with a **demonstration** makes misunderstandings visible while they’re still cheap to fix.'],
}
